  "duration_s": 2340.8,
  "findings": [
    "**Near-linear until memory runs out.** From 1M to 20.1M rows (20\u00d7 the data) the Bronze \u2192 Silver load took 25.6\u00d7 as long; throughput peaked at 195k rows/s (10M rows) on one laptop. The last step (10M \u2192 20.1M) took 3.7\u00d7 as long for 2\u00d7 the rows: a 16 GB machine starts spilling there, the point where a cluster (or Databricks) takes over.",
    "**Incremental MERGE beats rewriting.** At 20.1M rows an insert-only MERGE took 4.54 s, 25.2\u00d7 faster than the 114 s full rewrite. Scattered corrections forced MERGE to rewrite 16 files (92.88 s); with **deletion vectors** the same update rewrote 0 files and took 40.68 s (2.3\u00d7 faster than without).",
    "**Partitioning by month pays off for monthly reports.** The one-month report read 1 of 24 files on the month-partitioned table vs 16 of 16 unpartitioned (0.24 s vs 0.30 s, 1.3\u00d7 faster).",
    "**Z-ordering speeds up point lookups.** One customer's history read 2 of 178 files after Z-ordering by user_id vs 16 of 16 before (98 ms vs 0.39 s, 4.0\u00d7 faster). On the month-partitioned table the same lookup took 0.41 s: a layout helps the queries it was designed for.",
    "**Small files are a tax.** 25 tiny merges cost 160 s (6.4 s per commit) and grew the table from 16 to 416 files; OPTIMIZE (0.48 s) compacted it to 17, and a full scan went from 1.11 s to 0.83 s. Batch small loads and compact regularly.",
    "**Right-size the engine.** The same aggregation over 20.1M rows took 0.77 s in Spark and 36 ms in DuckDB (DuckDB 21.3\u00d7 faster). On one machine an in-process engine wins; Spark earns its overhead once data outgrows a node, which is why the warehouse and the admin console query with DuckDB.",
    "**Tune shuffle partitions.** Building Gold at 20.1M rows with adaptive execution off: 4 partitions 42.67 s, 16 partitions 47.04 s, 200 partitions 64.56 s. 4 was fastest here; the default 200 suits clusters, and AQE (on in the pipeline) adapts this automatically."
  ]
